."}, {"argument": "The sparse g-band detections from day -33 to day -0.5 cluster near 18 to 19.5 mag, broadly compatible with a luminous z around 0.2 stripped-envelope event sampled near peak with gaps. Its high Galactic latitude and host association favor an extragalactic explosion consistent with a massive-star origin.", "hypothesis": "ibc", "refuted_if": {"band": "ztfg", "day": 3, "direction": "above", "flux": 120, "why": "A month after the fitted peak a stripped-envelope supernova must be fading on its radioactive tail so persistent near-peak brightness rules it out."}, "weakest_point": "The fit is very poor with high chi2 per point and negligible weight, and rapid swings between detections and deep limits on few-day timescales plus stamp classification as bogus with p-real only six p"}, {"argument": "The g-band detections from day -33 to -0.5 show a ~1 mag rise followed by a ~30 day flat plateau near 18.1-18.4 mag, matching a hydrogen-recombination plateau. The very low-redshift fit places the peak at day -37.6 just before first light, consistent with a long-lived Type IIP caught on the rise.", "hypothesis": "ii", "refuted_if": {"band": "ztfg", "day": 3, "direction": "below", "flux": 80, "why": "A Type IIP on plateau at ~37 days past peak should still hold near 150-200 uJy and cannot fade by half in three days."}, "weakest_point": "The case is undermined by a chi2 per point of 14.82 with zero weight, a bogus stamp with p_real 0.06, deep g limits interleaved between bright detections and no r-band detections at all."}], "weights": {"ia": 0.7297, "null": 0.2703}, "wording": {"term": "unlikely", "text": "It is unlikely the distant flash will still look bright in greenish visible light 3 days from now.", "words_match": true}}, "prev": "43e251d687dfd66149a177c5dbc523b925fdc827af2375a016676a985625c504", "seq": 10, "time": "2024-11-13T16:02:29+00:00", "v": 2}
